1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:10,980
The second company he ate was the
one he needed.

2
00:00:11,550 --> 00:00:16,977
Aegis Dynamics built things that flew. Drones,
mostly — reconnaissance drones for the

3
00:00:16,997 --> 00:00:22,616
Army, strike drones for the Air Force, long-endurance
maritime drones for the Navy, and,

4
00:00:22,636 --> 00:00:28,384
since the war in Ukraine had taught every general on
Earth what small cheap machines could

5
00:00:28,404 --> 00:00:34,023
do, swarms of tiny ones that could be carried in a
backpack and launched by the hundred.

6
00:00:34,043 --> 00:00:39,470
It employed sixty thousand people, it earned most
of its money from the Pentagon, and

7
00:00:39,490 --> 00:00:41,777
it was worth eighty billion dollars.

8
00:00:42,347 --> 00:00:48,292
It was also the only company in America with a
delivery system good enough for what the

9
00:00:48,312 --> 00:00:52,268
Department of Defense had quietly
asked Umbrella to build.

10
00:00:53,838 --> 00:00:59,537
Martin Sloane's second contract had arrived in the
spring. It was an extension of the

11
00:00:59,557 --> 00:01:05,256
first. A vaccine that could be made in eight days
was useful, the Pentagon's planners

12
00:01:05,276 --> 00:01:11,379
had noted, only if it could be delivered in eight
hours — to a city, to a base, to a fleet,

13
00:01:11,399 --> 00:01:17,098
before a pathogen had time to spread. They wanted a
way to vaccinate a million people

14
00:01:17,118 --> 00:01:19,721
at once, from the air, without needles.

15
00:01:20,292 --> 00:01:26,228
Umbrella could make the vaccine. Aegis could build
the drones. The Pentagon wanted them

16
00:01:26,248 --> 00:01:28,282
to work together. So they did.

17
00:01:30,352 --> 00:01:36,425
Aegis Dynamics was run by General Thomas Wyatt,
United States Army, retired.

18
00:01:36,995 --> 00:01:43,061
He had commanded a division in Iraq and a corps in
Afghanistan, and had retired with four

19
00:01:43,081 --> 00:01:49,147
stars, and gone into industry because the Army's
pension did not pay for three children's

20
00:01:49,167 --> 00:01:55,371
college and a house in McLean. He was sixty-six. He
was a patriot of the old, uncomplicated

21
00:01:55,391 --> 00:02:01,525
kind — the kind that stands up for the anthem at ball
games and means it — and he disliked

22
00:02:01,545 --> 00:02:04,193
Albert Wesker from the moment they met.

23
00:02:04,763 --> 00:02:10,457
He could not have said why. He had shaken the man's
hand at the Pentagon, in a conference

24
00:02:10,477 --> 00:02:16,043
room full of colonels, and felt — he told his wife
that night — as though he had shaken

25
00:02:16,063 --> 00:02:21,243
hands with a very good salesman who was selling
something he did not want to buy.

26
00:02:21,933 --> 00:02:26,409
I'll be straight with you, Doctor. I don't like
working with people I can't read. I've

27
00:02:26,429 --> 00:02:29,650
spent forty years reading men across
tables. I can't read you.

28
00:02:30,170 --> 00:02:35,712
That's a compliment, General. Most people find me
very easy to read. They just read the

29
00:02:35,732 --> 00:02:36,542
wrong things.

30
00:02:37,062 --> 00:02:38,909
That's what worries me.

31
00:02:40,930 --> 00:02:46,978
They worked together anyway, for nine months,
because the Pentagon told them to. And in

32
00:02:46,998 --> 00:02:53,255
those nine months, Umbrella's engineers learned
everything about Aegis Dynamics that there

33
00:02:53,275 --> 00:02:59,183
was to learn: its factories, its supply chains, its
contracts, its people, its debts,

34
00:02:59,203 --> 00:03:01,067
and its one great weakness.

35
00:03:01,637 --> 00:03:06,661
Its one great weakness was Thomas Wyatt.
Because Thomas Wyatt was dying.

36
00:03:08,730 --> 00:03:14,294
He had told no one except his wife and his
oncologist. Pancreatic cancer, stage four,

37
00:03:14,314 --> 00:03:20,075
discovered in the spring of twenty thirty-two during
a routine check-up. Six months, the

38
00:03:20,095 --> 00:03:25,856
oncologist had said. Perhaps nine, with treatment. He
had not started treatment, because

39
00:03:25,876 --> 00:03:31,440
it would have meant telling his board, and telling
his board would have meant telling

40
00:03:31,460 --> 00:03:37,023
the market, and telling the market would have meant
watching the company he had spent

41
00:03:37,043 --> 00:03:42,081
eight years rebuilding sold off in pieces while
he was still alive to see it.

42
00:03:42,651 --> 00:03:48,318
The Director knew by the second meeting. The
Cold-Read did not need a scan. It had seen

43
00:03:48,338 --> 00:03:53,939
the faint yellow at the edge of the general's eyes,
and the way he held his coffee cup

44
00:03:53,959 --> 00:03:59,560
with his right hand pressed flat against his
stomach, and the flicker of pain he hid a

45
00:03:59,580 --> 00:04:01,063
dozen times in an hour.

46
00:04:03,133 --> 00:04:05,512
It was Evelyn Marsh who proposed it.

47
00:04:06,082 --> 00:04:11,400
She had been waiting for two years. The Progenitor
Initiative had learned a great deal

48
00:04:11,420 --> 00:04:16,676
from the five enhanced macaques, and from the grey
sleeper in the cold room, and from

49
00:04:16,696 --> 00:04:21,952
the blood of the man who had built it all. It had
learned how to make a derivative of

50
00:04:21,972 --> 00:04:27,413
the Prototype that a human body might tolerate — not
the full virus, not what was in the

51
00:04:27,433 --> 00:04:32,875
Director's blood, but a gentler strain, a shadow of
it, that would rewrite damaged cells

52
00:04:32,895 --> 00:04:34,986
and leave most healthy ones alone.

53
00:04:35,555 --> 00:04:36,334
Most.

54
00:04:37,023 --> 00:04:42,030
It will cure him. I'm almost certain it will cure
him. The tumour is exactly the kind

55
00:04:42,050 --> 00:04:44,574
of damaged tissue the strain
targets first.

56
00:04:45,095 --> 00:04:46,078
Almost certain.

57
00:04:46,596 --> 00:04:51,944
Eighty percent. Perhaps eighty-five. And he's dying
anyway, Albert. He has five months.

58
00:04:51,964 --> 00:04:53,548
What does he have to lose?

59
00:04:54,069 --> 00:04:55,308
And what else will it do?

60
00:04:56,827 --> 00:04:59,036
We don't know. That's why we need him.

61
00:05:01,055 --> 00:05:06,468
The Director looked at her for a long time. She was
thirty-three now. She had not left

62
00:05:06,488 --> 00:05:12,026
the Arklay sublevel for more than a day at a time in
four years. Her skin had the pallor

63
00:05:12,046 --> 00:05:17,521
of someone who lived under artificial light, and her
eyes had the brightness of someone

64
00:05:17,541 --> 00:05:22,132
who did not need to see the sun, because she
had seen something brighter.

65
00:05:22,704 --> 00:05:28,803
The Cold-Read showed him her face, and he read it, and
what he read there was not ambition,

66
00:05:28,823 --> 00:05:34,653
or even curiosity. It was love. A pure, total,
terrible love — not for him, anymore, or

67
00:05:34,673 --> 00:05:38,754
not only for him. For the work. For
what the work could make.

68
00:05:39,444 --> 00:05:41,430
She is magnificent.

69
00:05:41,949 --> 00:05:44,042
She's becoming something I didn't plan.

70
00:05:44,562 --> 00:05:46,910
The best ones always do.

71
00:05:48,930 --> 00:05:52,003
Ask him yourself, Evelyn. If he
says yes, it's yes.

72
00:05:54,523 --> 00:05:59,664
She met him on a bench by the Potomac, at night,
because he would not come to Delaware.

73
00:06:00,233 --> 00:06:05,804
She did not lie to him. That was her gift, and her
horror: she never lied to anyone about

74
00:06:05,824 --> 00:06:11,395
the work. She told him what the strain was, and what
it had done to twenty-four macaques,

75
00:06:11,415 --> 00:06:16,797
and what it might do to him. She told him eighty
percent. She told him that nobody had

76
00:06:16,817 --> 00:06:22,765
ever received it. She told him that he would have to
come to a facility that did not officially

77
00:06:22,785 --> 00:06:28,293
exist, and stay for twelve weeks, and that when he
came out he would be alive, and well,

78
00:06:28,313 --> 00:06:32,313
and that he might be — she chose the word
carefully — different.

79
00:06:33,004 --> 00:06:34,105
Different how?

80
00:06:34,625 --> 00:06:40,813
We don't know. The animals that survived were calmer.
More focused. Stronger. More — cooperative.

81
00:06:41,333 --> 00:06:43,194
Cooperative with who?

82
00:06:45,213 --> 00:06:46,699
With the people who treated them.

83
00:06:49,218 --> 00:06:54,784
Thomas Wyatt sat on the bench by the river for a long
time, looking at the lights of the

84
00:06:54,804 --> 00:06:59,353
Pentagon on the far bank, where he had spent
the best years of his life.

85
00:06:59,923 --> 00:07:05,211
He thought about his wife, Eleanor, whom he had
married in nineteen ninety. He thought

86
00:07:05,231 --> 00:07:10,643
about his three children, and the grandchild due in
March. He thought about five months,

87
00:07:10,663 --> 00:07:13,605
and what the last three of them
would look like.

88
00:07:14,175 --> 00:07:20,376
He was a brave man. He had been brave in Fallujah and
in Kandahar. But courage is a thing

89
00:07:20,396 --> 00:07:26,597
you use against an enemy, and there was no enemy on
the bench by the river — only a quiet

90
00:07:26,617 --> 00:07:30,442
young woman with a folder, offering
him his grandchild.

91
00:07:31,132 --> 00:07:32,185
All right.

92
00:07:34,704 --> 00:07:40,861
General Thomas Wyatt took medical leave from Aegis
Dynamics on the first of October, twenty

93
00:07:40,881 --> 00:07:46,834
thirty-two. The company's announcement said he was
recovering from a minor procedure and

94
00:07:46,854 --> 00:07:53,350
would return early in the new year. His wife was told he
was at a private clinic in Switzerland.

95
00:07:53,370 --> 00:07:59,188
He was not in Switzerland. He was a hundred feet
beneath a brick building on the south

96
00:07:59,208 --> 00:08:05,025
bank of the Christina River, in a white room at the
end of a white corridor, next door

97
00:08:05,045 --> 00:08:08,147
to a cold room where something
enormous slept.

98
00:08:08,718 --> 00:08:13,681
The tumour was gone in nineteen days. Evelyn
cried when she saw the scan.

99
00:08:14,252 --> 00:08:19,550
The other changes took longer. His hair, which had
been silver for fifteen years, began

100
00:08:19,570 --> 00:08:25,052
at the roots to grow back dark. His skin tightened.
The arthritis in his hands, from forty

101
00:08:25,072 --> 00:08:30,492
years of cold mornings, disappeared. His heart rate
fell to forty beats a minute at rest,

102
00:08:30,512 --> 00:08:35,749
and stayed there. By the eighth week he could lift
twice what he had lifted at thirty.

103
00:08:35,769 --> 00:08:41,006
By the tenth, he had stopped sleeping more than two
hours a night, and did not seem to

104
00:08:41,026 --> 00:08:41,495
need to.

105
00:08:42,066 --> 00:08:47,199
And by the twelfth week, when the Director came
down to see him, he stood up when the

106
00:08:47,219 --> 00:08:48,776
Director entered the room.

107
00:08:49,346 --> 00:08:55,508
Not the way a man stands for a guest. The way a
soldier stands for a commanding officer

108
00:08:55,528 --> 00:09:01,761
— instantly, completely, without deciding to. His
back straight, his hands at his sides,

109
00:09:01,781 --> 00:09:02,970
his eyes forward.

110
00:09:03,659 --> 00:09:04,612
Director.

111
00:09:06,633 --> 00:09:12,269
The Director looked at him for a long time. Viral
Dominion, the new sense, the sound at

112
00:09:12,289 --> 00:09:17,860
the edge of hearing — it was humming. It could feel
the strain in the general's blood,

113
00:09:17,880 --> 00:09:23,841
the way it could feel the sleeper in the cold room. A
thread. A thin, bright thread, running

114
00:09:23,861 --> 00:09:25,922
from the old soldier to himself.

115
00:09:26,611 --> 00:09:28,020
How do you feel, General?

116
00:09:28,541 --> 00:09:32,041
Very well, Director. Thank you. I'm
ready to go back to work.

117
00:09:32,561 --> 00:09:34,269
And what will you do when you get there?

118
00:09:34,788 --> 00:09:36,748
Whatever you think is best.

119
00:09:39,267 --> 00:09:44,132
A general who obeys. Do you understand
what she has given you?

120
00:09:44,653 --> 00:09:45,331
Yes.

121
00:09:45,850 --> 00:09:51,713
In my whole life I never had one. Not one. They all
thought they knew better. They all

122
00:09:51,733 --> 00:09:58,213
waited for me to fail. They tried to kill me in my own
headquarters with a bomb in a briefcase.

123
00:09:58,233 --> 00:10:03,345
And she has made one, in twelve weeks, in a
white room, out of a dying man.

124
00:10:05,365 --> 00:10:11,243
Wesker did not answer. He was looking at the general's
eyes. They were a little too bright.

125
00:10:11,263 --> 00:10:16,883
They caught the light the way the macaques' eyes had
caught it, in the glass enclosure,

126
00:10:16,903 --> 00:10:18,827
when Owen Carrick walked past.

127
00:10:19,397 --> 00:10:20,958
And they did not blink.

128
00:10:23,528 --> 00:10:29,556
General Thomas Wyatt returned to Aegis Dynamics in
the second week of January. His board,

129
00:10:29,576 --> 00:10:35,468
which had been preparing quietly to replace him,
found him in the boardroom at seven in

130
00:10:35,488 --> 00:10:41,244
the morning, twenty pounds lighter, with dark hair,
and a grip that made the chairman

131
00:10:41,264 --> 00:10:41,652
wince.

132
00:10:42,221 --> 00:10:48,454
In February, Umbrella Life Sciences made an offer to
acquire Aegis Dynamics for ninety-four

133
00:10:48,474 --> 00:10:54,500
billion dollars in cash. The board — expecting their
chief executive to fight it the way

134
00:10:54,520 --> 00:11:00,409
he had fought everything in his career — called a
special meeting of the shareholders.

135
00:11:00,980 --> 00:11:06,091
General Wyatt stood at the podium, and smiled,
and recommended that they accept.

136
00:11:06,660 --> 00:11:12,528
He smiled a great deal that day. That was what people
remembered afterwards. He had never

137
00:11:12,548 --> 00:11:18,216
been a man who smiled much. Now he smiled at
everyone — at the board, at the analysts,

138
00:11:18,236 --> 00:11:23,839
at the shareholders who asked hostile questions —
and he held each smile a little too

139
00:11:23,859 --> 00:11:27,345
long, until the person he was
smiling at looked away.

140
00:11:27,914 --> 00:11:34,748
The shareholders accepted. The Pentagon, advised by a
very small office that Martin Sloane

141
00:11:34,768 --> 00:11:41,296
had spoken to, raised no objection. The Committee on
Foreign Investment had nothing to

142
00:11:41,316 --> 00:11:47,007
review, because Umbrella was an American
company. The deal closed in April.

143
00:11:49,827 --> 00:11:58,460
Corporate conquest. Aegis Dynamics. Personnel
conversion, Progenitor, human, one.

144
00:12:00,049 --> 00:12:03,198
Personnel conversion.
Progenitor. Human. One.

145
00:12:03,769 --> 00:12:09,258
The Director read that line three times. It was a
very small number. He knew, reading

146
00:12:09,278 --> 00:12:11,397
it, that it would not stay small.

147
00:12:13,967 --> 00:12:17,903
Eleanor Wyatt had been married to
Thomas for forty-two years.

148
00:12:18,474 --> 00:12:23,706
She had nursed him through two wars and a divorce
that almost happened and a heart scare

149
00:12:23,726 --> 00:12:28,839
in twenty-nineteen. She had sat with him in the
oncologist's office in the spring, and

150
00:12:28,859 --> 00:12:34,032
held his hand, and watched him decide not to tell
anyone. And in January she had opened

151
00:12:34,052 --> 00:12:39,105
her front door to a man with her husband's face and
her husband's voice and dark hair

152
00:12:39,125 --> 00:12:44,358
and a grip that did not hurt her only because he was
careful, and he had kissed her, and

153
00:12:44,378 --> 00:12:46,507
smiled, and held the smile too long.

154
00:12:47,076 --> 00:12:53,322
He was kind to her. He was attentive. He remembered
every anniversary and every grandchild's

155
00:12:53,342 --> 00:12:59,180
birthday. He no longer drank, or swore, or shouted
at the Commanders on television. He

156
00:12:59,200 --> 00:13:05,105
did not sleep. At three in the morning she would
find him standing at the window of the

157
00:13:05,125 --> 00:13:08,442
dark living room, perfectly
still, looking north.

158
00:13:09,133 --> 00:13:10,628
Tom? What are you looking at?

159
00:13:11,148 --> 00:13:13,447
Nothing, Ellie. Go back to bed.

160
00:13:15,966 --> 00:13:21,698
She did not go back to bed. She sat in the dark and
watched him, and understood — the

161
00:13:21,718 --> 00:13:27,584
way wives of forty-two years understand things,
without evidence, completely — that the

162
00:13:27,604 --> 00:13:31,440
cancer had been cured, and her
husband had not come home.

163
00:13:32,010 --> 00:13:38,049
In April, she found a business card on a nightstand
in a hotel in Washington, where she

164
00:13:38,069 --> 00:13:44,107
had gone for a fundraiser. It was not hers. Someone
had left it for her. It had an Oslo

165
00:13:44,127 --> 00:13:46,544
telephone number on it, and a name.

166
00:13:47,115 --> 00:13:48,616
Ingrid Halvorsen.
